  "body": "# Associated User Story\n\nAs a manager, I want a separate table under the members table that contains admins and all their information, so that I can see admin profile details in one place without admins being duplicated in the members list.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n\n### The dashboard shows a separate admins table below the members table, listing each admin and their full information, with admins no longer appearing in the members table\n\n**Sunny:** A manager opens the dashboard and sees the members table with a separate admins table directly beneath it. The admins table lists each admin along with their profile details (name, email, role, expected graduation, PT school application term, carpool availability, and phone number if they opted in). Admins appear only in the admins table and not in the members table. When someone is made an admin, they move from the members table to the admins table, and if they are changed back, they move back.\n\n**Rainy:** The admin data fails to load, there are no admins yet, or a user without permission tries to view the admins table. The system shows a clear message (e.g., \"Admins could not be loaded, try again later\" or \"No admins to display\") instead of a blank table or wrong information, and the members table still loads normally. A phone number is never shown for an admin who hasn't opted in, and only admins and managers can see the admins table.\n\n# Definition of Done\n\nA story is done when its acceptance criteria are met and every item in the team PR template is satisfied:\n(A) code review by a non-author, coding standard met, RuboCop checked with output/CI link, and setup changes documented;\n(B) the human-only maintainability checks (meaningful comments and names, skinny controllers, thin views, DRY, Rails conventions, foreign keys, enums, nested routes);\n(C) tests added or updated and passing. Any deferred items or RuboCop exclusions are explained in Section D of the PR.",
